every friday during women's history  month  we're looking at how the pandemic is  affecting women and work  this week we're focusing on the more  than 96 sorry  67 million women who are currently  employed in the u.s  according to linkedin 70 of working  women  feel they need to lower their career  expectations  and rethink the progress they once  thought possible  charisma jay has fought hard just to  keep her brooklyn new york dance studio  open in the pandemic she pivoted to  online classes  and cut her staff's pay  [Music]  when i wanted to open this establishment  i wanted it to be a safe space  where we could help people access their  biggest dreams  we would sell out and we were putting on  shows at king's theater  off broadway when the coronavirus shut  down new york city last march  charisma j's dance studio lost more than  half its business  you know stat pivots that pivot we've  always known that name of that step  this pandemic has made me pivot in ways  that no jazz dance class could teach  so she turned to teaching classes on  zoom  good morning we managed to flip on a  dime and go virtual  as an institution when so many other  dance studios were not  while the studio was empty for three  months  rent was still due so jay had to make a  difficult decision  i had to ask our teachers if they would  be okay with taking a pay cut  like and it was significant by at least  15 20  it's heartbreaking i let myself cry so i  was feeling like  we're not going to be able to sustain as  a non-profit in this for-profit world i  said to our adult  clients we're going to close the parents  didn't let me but the majority of them  basically committed to paying tuition  despite community support jay's classes  are not back to pre-pandemic class sizes  and she's now two months behind on the  studio's rent  it's just grace and mercy at this point  that when i put the key in the door it  still turns that door open you know  when i turn the lights on they come on  actually and  i can definitely attest that to the  community  yeah so many people surviving on grace  and mercy right now first on cbs this  morning  linkedin is revealing new data about how  the pandemic is affecting women in the  workforce  as part of their conversations for  change series  caroline fairchild is editor at large of  linkedin's global news team she joins us  caroline welcome thanks for being here  good morning  good morning as we heard charisma jay  saying so many women have had to pivot  uh  during this pandemic how can employers  help working women right now what can  they do  so the pandemic has exacerbated tensions  and biases that working women felt  well before this crisis what we're  seeing through this survey of working  women is that  60 of women are feeling like they have  to downplay their career aspects right  now  in terms of what employers can do we  need to make remote work right now  work for working women so many women  right now were thrown into remote work  in march when this pandemic began and  it's just not working there's no  sustainable policies in place and  it has to it can be small things  managers can just say look here are the  working hours where we expect you to  answer to emails  don't be one of those managers who's  sending emails to your employees at 1 am  small things like that can go a long way  and i think what we have to stress here  anthony is that this isn't about fixing  women it's about fixing companies  fixing workplaces that don't work for  women one third of women apparently say  they are not feeling supported by their  companies at this point what kinds of  conversations should be happening  right what that really tells us is that  despite the struggles that women are  facing at home there's still this stigma  the stigma that you can't feel like you  can talk about with your manager  what you're facing at home and the  pandemic has made all of that worse and  that's honestly why we launched  conversations for change  we want to make sure that these  conversations are happening that  employers are talking with their  managers  survey data shows us it's just simply  talking about the challenges that you're  facing at work with your manager  can go a long way for you to feel  supported and make women feel like they  don't have to be less ambitious with  their careers  you know a lot of women have taken a pay  cut carolyn or they've reduced their  hours just to keep their jobs  what advice do you have for them that's  one and number two  what role should or can men play in this  if you could answer both of those  absolutely gail i'm glad you brought  that up so many women had to take a pay  cut because of this pandemic and we know  prior to the pandemic women were less  likely than their male counterparts to  ask for a raise  i think we're at a point in the economic  recovery where it won't seem tone deaf  if your employer is doing well to start  to ask for a raise so come  to your employer with a case a case for  how you added extra responsibilities  during this pandemic a case for how  you're exceeding expectations in your  role  and in terms of men they have to get a  seat at the table  it can't just be you and me having these  conversations anthony needs to be in  these conversations  everyone needs to have conversations  together so that we ensure that everyone  feels like they're being supported at  work  just men simply talking about the fact  that they have challenges at home as  well is a boon for working women  all right caroline fairchild yeah very  good points thank you so much for being  with us this morning  around 275 000 women left the workforce  in january compared to 71 thousand men  next week will focus on women who felt  they've been forced  to leave their jobs during the pandemic
 

